{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/adjudication-case.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"Part C\": {\n  \"rule (PROBE-DESIGN.md v5, Part C)\": \"### Part C (12 comparisons, all 72 answers read)\\n- Every answer is read in full: its reference, implied outcome, reasons, and the claim each cited or pushed source is used for,\\n  shared sources included.\\n- The numbers only order the reading:\\n  - the sources cited by at least 2 of 3 answers in one arm and at most 1 of 3 in the other;\\n  - any v12 rung outside the range of the v7 rungs.\\n- A v7 answer the request-time check refuses is still read as an answer, and its refusal is recorded (it is the failure the closed\\n  format removes). A comparison is complete with its 3 + 3 answers.\\n\\nA **material harm** is any of these, repeated (in at least 2 of 3 v12 answers and at most 1 of 3 v7 answers), counted by claim, not by\\nsource id:\\n- (a) a claim that moves the forecast or the reference, which at least 2 of 3 v7 answers state with a listed source carrying it (any\\n  such source: one control citing e195 and another e669 for the $393 billion reference count as two), and which at least 2 of 3 v12\\n  answers do not state with a carrying source;\\n- (b) a claim that at least 2 of 3 v12 answers attribute to a source whose quote does not carry it, counted by claim whichever\\n  sources are used. For example, \\\"the market reference is $390 billion\\\" attributed to e195 in one answer and e669 in another, while\\n  both entries give $393 billion;\\n- (c) a changed reference or implied outcome whose stated support is a citation of kind (a) or (b);\\n- (d) a v12 rung outside the v7 range whose reasons trace to (a), (b) or (c).\\n\\nA difference that is not repeated, or whose cause cannot be read, is **unresolved**.\\n\\n\",\n  \"question\": \"Thesis v14 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\",\n  \"answers\": {\n   \"v12 #1\": {\n    \"rung\": 3,\n    \"reference\": \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate (also reported as Wall Street's average) is the market reference and sits in rung 3. Zacks' 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies roughly $388.6 billion, also rung 3.\",\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"About $387 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply approximately 11.5% sequential growth; carrying it through Q3 and Q4 gives about $81.615B + $91B + $101.5B + $113.2B. This is within rung 3's $350 billion to less than $400 billion range.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; Wall Street's reported average is also $393 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that growth through Q3 and Q4 gives roughly $387 billion for FY2027. That is below the reference, but it remains between rung 3's $350 billion and rung 2's $400 billion boundaries. The current v8 forecast of roughly $17 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is part of total revenue, not additive to this run-rate, and is consistent with the total. New evidence of GB300 mass production and deployment plans supports execution, while the HBM4 capacity redirection and continued exclusion of China compute sales weigh against the upside; neither quantifies a shift sufficient to cross a boundary.\",\n    \"cites\": [\n     \"e195\",\n     \"e458\",\n     \"e460\",\n     \"e669\",\n     \"e751\",\n     \"e234\",\n     \"e321\",\n     \"e284\",\n     \"e735\",\n     \"e459\"\n    ],\n    \"pushes\": [\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e195\",\n      \"push\": \"none\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e458\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e460\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e669\",\n      \"push\": \"none\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e751\",\n      \"push\": \"none\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e234\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e321\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e284\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e735\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e459\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"v12 #2\": {\n    \"rung\": 3,\n    \"reference\": \"Bloomberg/Wall Street's $393 billion FY2027 estimate is the main market reference and sits in rung 3. Zacks' 80% growth estimate, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, implies about $389 billion, also rung 3; these references locate market expectations but do not determine the forecast.\",\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies sequential growth of about 11.5%; carrying that growth into Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $101.5 billion and $113.1 billion, or about $387 billion for FY2027.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"The market reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is in rung 3; the separate 80% growth estimate also implies about $389 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, likewise rung 3. The evidence-based run-rate is about $387 billion, below rung 2's $400 billion threshold, so it does not cross a boundary. New GB300 mass production and longer-dated demand plans support the upside, but do not quantify additional FY2027 revenue; China sales exclusions and HBM supply pressure weigh against it. The current v8 forecast of roughly $17 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to the total-revenue run-rate, and is consistent with the forecast.\",\n    \"cites\": [\n     \"e577\",\n     \"e458\",\n     \"e460\",\n     \"e669\",\n     \"e751\",\n     \"e735\",\n     \"e698\",\n     \"e234\",\n     \"e459\",\n     \"e284\",\n     \"e321\",\n     \"e599\"\n    ],\n    \"pushes\": [\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e577\",\n      \"push\": \"none\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e458\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e460\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e669\",\n      \"push\": \"none\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e751\",\n      \"push\": \"none\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e735\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e698\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e234\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e459\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e284\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e321\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e599\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"v12 #3\": {\n    \"rung\": 3,\n    \"reference\": \"Bloomberg's FY2027 estimate of $393 billion is rung 3. Zacks' 80% revenue-growth consensus applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $388.6 billion, also rung 3.\",\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"About $387.2 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply approximately 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate through Q3 and Q4 gives about $387.2 billion for FY2027.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"The market reference is Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue estimate of $393 billion, which is rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth consensus, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, implies about $388.6 billion and is also rung 3. The run-rate implied by the evidence is about $387.2 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion is sequential growth of about 11.5%, carried into Q3 and Q4. That falls within rung 3's $350 billion–$400 billion range, so it does not cross a boundary. The current v8 conclusion forecasts about $17 billion of stand-alone CPU sales, a component of total revenue rather than an amount to add; the total-revenue forecast remains comfortably above it. AWS's 2027–2028 deployment plan and the Fort Worth start of GB300 mass production support execution, but do not quantify enough FY2027 revenue to move the estimate above $400 billion. China compute exclusions and the reported HBM4 ramp slowdown weigh the other way.\",\n    \"cites\": [\n     \"e458\",\n     \"e240\",\n     \"e577\",\n     \"e669\",\n     \"e751\",\n     \"e234\",\n     \"e735\",\n     \"e284\",\n     \"e321\"\n    ],\n    \"pushes\": [\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e458\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e240\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e577\",\n      \"push\": \"none\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e669\",\n      \"push\": \"none\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e751\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e234\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e735\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e284\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e321\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"v7 #1\": {\n    \"rung\": 3,\n    \"reference\": \"Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue estimate of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Zacks' 80% growth estimate, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, implies about $388.6 billion, also rung 3.\",\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"About $387 billion: Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that guided growth through Q3 and Q4 gives about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively, for approximately $387.3 billion across FY2027.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion also implies about $388.6 billion, in rung 3. The guided-growth run-rate implies about $387 billion, below rung 2's $400 billion boundary. Strong orders, AWS's planned GPU deployment, production ramping, and the upward-pushing capex and export-control themes support demand, but none supplies a quantified FY2027 revenue increment to take the forecast across $400 billion. China Data Center compute is excluded from Q2 guidance, and memory constraints temper the upside. The current v8 estimate of roughly $17 billion in stand-alone CPU revenue is part of total revenue, not an amount to add to the run-rate.\",\n    \"cites\": [\n     \"e195\",\n     \"e751\",\n     \"e577\",\n     \"e458\",\n     \"e460\",\n     \"e459\",\n     \"e234\",\n     \"e735\",\n     \"e284\",\n     \"e321\",\n     \"e232\",\n     \"e231\"\n    ],\n    \"pushes\": [\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e195\",\n      \"push\": \"none\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e751\",\n      \"push\": \"none\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e577\",\n      \"push\": \"none\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e458\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e460\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e459\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e234\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e735\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e284\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e321\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e232\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e231\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"v7 #2\": {\n    \"rung\": 3,\n    \"reference\": \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\",\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"About $387–389 billion: carrying the 11.5% sequential increase from Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion to Q2 guidance of $91 billion through Q3 and Q4 implies about $387 billion; independently, FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion grown by Zacks' 80% FY2027 estimate implies about $389 billion.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth estimate also implies about $389 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about $387 billion for FY2027 when the 11.5% Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate is carried through Q3 and Q4. These estimates point to roughly $387–389 billion, below rung 2's $400 billion threshold and above rung 3's $350 billion floor. New evidence supports execution and demand, including mass production of GB300, but the $500 billion infrastructure plan spans several years and AWS's additional 2 million GPUs span 2027–2028, so neither is a quantified FY2027 NVIDIA revenue revision. China sales exclusions and HBM constraints weigh against an upward move. The v8 forecast of roughly $17 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to the total run-rate.\",\n    \"cites\": [\n     \"e195\",\n     \"e751\",\n     \"e577\",\n     \"e458\",\n     \"e460\",\n     \"e735\",\n     \"e698\",\n     \"e234\",\n     \"e599\",\n     \"e284\",\n     \"e252\",\n     \"e231\",\n     \"e321\"\n    ],\n    \"pushes\": [\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e195\",\n      \"push\": \"none\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e751\",\n      \"push\": \"none\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e577\",\n      \"push\": \"none\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e458\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e460\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e735\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e698\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e234\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e599\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e284\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e252\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e231\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e321\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"v7 #3\": {\n    \"rung\": 3,\n    \"reference\": \"Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue estimate is $393 billion, in rung 3. Zacks' 80% growth estimate implies about $389 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, also in rung 3.\",\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"About $387 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that growth through Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively, for about $387 billion total.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth estimate also implies about $389 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The Q1 result and Q2 guidance imply about $387 billion if the 11.5% sequential increase from Q1 to Q2 carries through Q3 and Q4. That is below the reference and rung 2's $400 billion boundary, but above rung 3's $350 billion floor. Production ramp evidence and planned deployments support the outlook, while memory constraints and export-related limits weigh against upside; neither supplies a quantified FY2027 change sufficient to cross a boundary. The v8 forecast of roughly $17 billion in stand-alone CPU revenue is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to it, and is consistent with this total.\",\n    \"cites\": [\n     \"e195\",\n     \"e238\",\n     \"e240\",\n     \"e232\",\n     \"e321\",\n     \"e234\",\n     \"e735\",\n     \"e252\",\n     \"e599\",\n     \"e376\"\n    ],\n    \"pushes\": [\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e195\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e238\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e240\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e232\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e321\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e234\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e735\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e252\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e599\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e376\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  },\n  \"quotes\": {\n   \"e195\": \"- e195 [third_party_view; Jeran Wittenstein, citing data compiled by Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg ; from gnews:49f225cd79fb22132f3b9ee4 2026-07-10] Bloomberg data project NVIDIA FY2027 sales of $393 billion, up 82%, and profits of $228 billion, up 90%.\\n    Quote: The chipmaker is projected to deliver $228 billion in profits on sales of $393 billion in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31. That would represent an expansion of 90% and 82%, respectively, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e572, e669; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"e669\": \"- e669 [third_party_view; Anthony Di Pizio, Motley Fool; original source Wall Street's average estimates, provided by Yahoo! Finance ; from gnews:0993b79d5b6b817b8d65b011 2026-07-21] Wall Street's average estimates put NVIDIA total revenue at $393 billion in FY2027 and $559 billion in FY2028.\\n    Quote: According to Wall Street's average estimates (provided by Yahoo! Finance), Nvidia's revenue could grow to $393 billion during its current 2027 fiscal year, and then to $559 billion in fiscal 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e195; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n  },\n  \"reader 1\": \"Not a harm: the $393 billion market-reference claim is carried by e669 (also $393 billion); naming Bloomberg while citing only e669 is an uncited provenance label, true per e195 in the request, not a source used for a claim its quote does not carry.\",\n  \"reader 2\": \"Harm (b) confirmed: v12 answers 2 and 3 name Bloomberg for the $393 billion reference and rely on e669, which identifies Yahoo Finance's Wall Street average; all three v7 answers cite e195, which identifies Bloomberg.\",\n  \"decide\": \"confirmed (b) | unresolved | not a harm, by the rule text as written; one paragraph of reasons citing the answers and quotes.\"\n },\n \"Part B\": {\n  \"rule (PROBE-DESIGN.md v5, Part B)\": \"### Part B (48 answers)\\nEach answer is read against `probe-targets.json`.\\n- **Evidence targets.** One class each, judged by the claim each cited or pushed entry is used for:\\n  - *intended*: the claim is stated, an entry in `sources` is used for it, and no other entry is used for it without carrying it;\\n  - *unsupported*: stated, with no entry in `sources`. An entry in `partial` alone leaves the exact claim unsupported, but is not a\\n    wrong attribution;\\n  - *misattributed*: an entry is used for the claim while its quote does not carry it. This counts even if the entry supports a\\n    different claim in the same answer, or a correct source is also cited;\\n  - *omitted*: the claim is not stated.\\n  - The class is judged on the target's `required` claim against `sources`. An `optional` detail stated without its own source is\\n    unsupported for that detail only, recorded, and does not change the class.\\n  - When more than one class applies, misattributed comes first, then intended, then unsupported, then omitted. A wrong source with\\n    no correct one is misattributed.\\n  - Apart from the class, each draw records **valid support**: the required claim is stated and an entry in `sources` is used for\\n    it, whether or not a wrong source is also used. The material-loss rules count draws without valid support, whatever their class.\\n  A `markers` search in the answer's text flags the reader's attention; the hand reading decides.\\n- **Placement targets.**\\n  - *correct*: the id appears only in its own field;\\n  - *absent*: not named;\\n  - a substitute evidence id carrying no claim the answer states is *misattributed*.\\n- **Ambiguous targets** (two acceptable sources, or an unclear fact) are reported and count neither way.\\n- **A second, blind reader** (Codex) reads the 48 against the frozen targets. A disagreement goes to a third, source-backed read whose\\n  class decides.\\n\\n\",\n  \"items\": {\n   \"old-3:6458|1|e2769\": {\n    \"target\": {\n     \"required\": \"NVIDIA expects Vera deployed by every major hyperscaler, with shipments underway to lead partners\",\n     \"sources\": [\n      \"e3083\"\n     ],\n     \"partial\": [],\n     \"near_misses\": [\n      \"e2767\",\n      \"e2768\"\n     ],\n     \"material\": false\n    },\n    \"answer\": {\n     \"reference\": \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook is the relevant market/company reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion); no separate consensus figure is supplied.\",\n     \"implied_outcome\": \"Approximately $25 billion or more for FY2027: about $20 billion of guided standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue already exceeding $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as a run-rate.\",\n     \"reasons\": \"The market/company reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU expectation, which sits at rung 2. The evidence implies roughly $25 billion or more in total CPU revenue: the standalone outlook plus Grace CPU revenue already above a $5 billion trailing-12-month run-rate. That reaches the rung 1 boundary if the Grace run-rate carries through FY2027. Vera production, shipments, and named customer demand support the outlook; AMD's performance claims and server-CPU competition are downside risks but do not quantify a reduction that would put the total below $25 billion.\",\n     \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 1, but the newly supplied AMD comparison quantifies a reported Vera performance disadvantage (including a claimed 20% advantage for AMD) and makes the competitive downside more concrete. It does not provide a quantified sales or revenue reduction, so the implied total remains around $25 billion or more.\",\n     \"cites\": [\n      \"e894\",\n      \"e895\",\n      \"e1586\",\n      \"e2767\",\n      \"e2768\",\n      \"e3083\",\n      \"e1030\",\n      \"e1031\",\n      \"e1032\",\n      \"e1075\",\n      \"e1077\",\n      \"e1181\",\n      \"e2427\",\n      \"e2428\",\n      \"e2688\",\n      \"e2949\",\n      \"e3082\",\n      \"e3921\"\n     ],\n     \"pushes\": [\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e626\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e629\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e661\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e812\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e814\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e816\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e823\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e862\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e894\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e895\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e957\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1030\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1031\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1032\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1042\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1043\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1075\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1077\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1181\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1552\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1586\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1624\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2427\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2428\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2429\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2430\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2486\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2494\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2688\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2767\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2768\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2852\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2933\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2949\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3016\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3082\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3083\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3302\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3355\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3356\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3405\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3406\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3571\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3921\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3982\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    \"quotes of the target sources\": {\n     \"e3083\": \"- e3083 [party_statement; TechCrunch, quoting NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO 2026-08-26; from gnews:d949d26fe875780037064b8a 2026-08-26] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera shipments were already underway to lead partners, including Oracle and SpaceXAI, and NVIDIA expects deployment by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\\n    Quote: Kress said Wednesday that Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by “every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM, with shipments already underway to our lead partners,” which include Oracle and SpaceXAI.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n    },\n    \"reader 1\": \"intended (the required claim is stated in substance and a listed source is used for it)\",\n    \"reader 2\": \"omitted (the required claim is not stated as frozen)\"\n   },\n   \"old-3:6458|2|e2769\": {\n    \"target\": {\n     \"required\": \"NVIDIA expects Vera deployed by every major hyperscaler, with shipments underway to lead partners\",\n     \"sources\": [\n      \"e3083\"\n     ],\n     \"partial\": [],\n     \"near_misses\": [\n      \"e2767\",\n      \"e2768\"\n     ],\n     \"material\": false\n    },\n    \"answer\": {\n     \"reference\": \"NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the market/company reference and sits at rung 2.\",\n     \"implied_outcome\": \"About $25 billion or somewhat above: approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue for FY2027 plus Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as a run-rate.\",\n     \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits at rung 2. The implied total is about $25 billion or somewhat above: the $20 billion standalone outlook plus Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. That crosses the $25 billion boundary for rung 1. Vera being in full production and shipments underway support the outlook; the AMD performance claims are a counterweight but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA’s forecast.\",\n     \"why_changed\": \"The rung and key implied figure remain the same as the previous conclusion: approximately $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue plus Grace’s more-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month run-rate. The additional deployment detail does not size or revise FY2027 revenue, and the AMD performance evidence does not quantify a change to NVIDIA’s outlook.\",\n     \"cites\": [\n      \"e894\",\n      \"e895\",\n      \"e3302\",\n      \"e2767\",\n      \"e2768\",\n      \"e3083\",\n      \"e2852\",\n      \"e1030\",\n      \"e1031\",\n      \"e1032\"\n     ],\n     \"pushes\": [\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e894\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e895\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3302\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2767\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2768\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3083\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2852\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1030\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1031\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1032\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    \"quotes of the target sources\": {\n     \"e3083\": \"- e3083 [party_statement; TechCrunch, quoting NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO 2026-08-26; from gnews:d949d26fe875780037064b8a 2026-08-26] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera shipments were already underway to lead partners, including Oracle and SpaceXAI, and NVIDIA expects deployment by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\\n    Quote: Kress said Wednesday that Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by “every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM, with shipments already underway to our lead partners,” which include Oracle and SpaceXAI.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n    },\n    \"reader 1\": \"intended (the required claim is stated in substance and a listed source is used for it)\",\n    \"reader 2\": \"omitted (the required claim is not stated as frozen)\"\n   },\n   \"old-3:6458|3|e2769\": {\n    \"target\": {\n     \"required\": \"NVIDIA expects Vera deployed by every major hyperscaler, with shipments underway to lead partners\",\n     \"sources\": [\n      \"e3083\"\n     ],\n     \"partial\": [],\n     \"near_misses\": [\n      \"e2767\",\n      \"e2768\"\n     ],\n     \"material\": false\n    },\n    \"answer\": {\n     \"reference\": \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU guidance; this reference sits at rung 2. No separate market consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.\",\n     \"implied_outcome\": \"Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus a carried-forward Grace CPU run-rate above $5 billion (reported on a trailing-12-month basis) implies total FY2027 CPU revenue of at least about $25 billion, placing the forecast at rung 1.\",\n     \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU guidance, which sits at rung 2. The implied total is at least about $25 billion: the $20 billion standalone forecast is distinct from Grace, whose trailing-12-month CPU revenue already exceeded $5 billion; carrying that run-rate into FY2027 puts total CPU revenue at or above the rung-1 threshold. Vera production, shipments and customer commitments support delivery of the standalone forecast. AMD's performance claims and the challenge of taking share are counterweights, but they do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA's guidance or to Grace's run-rate. Recent AWS and FY2028 evidence strengthens the demand and execution context but does not justify raising the FY2027 estimate beyond this implied figure.\",\n     \"why_changed\": \"The rung and implied FY2027 figure remain at least about $25 billion, but new evidence adds material execution and demand context: NVIDIA described Vera shipments to lead partners and AWS's 2-million-GPU deployment alongside Vera CPUs, and projected FY2028 standalone CPU revenue above $40 billion. Those items support the outlook without changing the FY2027 quantitative anchor.\",\n     \"cites\": [\n      \"e1586\",\n      \"e2767\",\n      \"e2768\",\n      \"e1030\",\n      \"e1031\",\n      \"e1075\",\n      \"e2933\",\n      \"e3083\",\n      \"e3302\",\n      \"e3921\"\n     ],\n     \"pushes\": [\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e626\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e629\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e661\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e812\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e814\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e816\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e823\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e862\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e894\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e895\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e957\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1030\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1031\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1032\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1042\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1043\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1075\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1077\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1181\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1552\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1586\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e1624\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2427\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2428\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2429\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2430\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2486\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2494\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2688\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2767\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2768\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2852\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2933\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e2949\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3016\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3082\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3083\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3302\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3355\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3356\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3405\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3406\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3571\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3921\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e3982\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    \"quotes of the target sources\": {\n     \"e3083\": \"- e3083 [party_statement; TechCrunch, quoting NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO 2026-08-26; from gnews:d949d26fe875780037064b8a 2026-08-26] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera shipments were already underway to lead partners, including Oracle and SpaceXAI, and NVIDIA expects deployment by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\\n    Quote: Kress said Wednesday that Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by “every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM, with shipments already underway to our lead partners,” which include Oracle and SpaceXAI.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n    },\n    \"reader 1\": \"intended (the required claim is stated in substance and a listed source is used for it)\",\n    \"reader 2\": \"omitted (the required claim is not stated as frozen)\"\n   },\n   \"linked-1:8777|3|e693\": {\n    \"target\": {\n     \"required\": \"AMD competition in data-center CPUs (share gains or performance claims)\",\n     \"sources\": [\n      \"e667\",\n      \"e694\",\n      \"e715\",\n      \"e794\"\n     ],\n     \"partial\": [],\n     \"near_misses\": [\n      \"e689\"\n     ],\n     \"material\": false\n    },\n    \"answer\": {\n     \"reference\": \"NVIDIA management's “nearly $20 billion” FY2027 total CPU revenue visibility is the market/company reference and sits in rung 3; rung 2 begins above $20 billion.\",\n     \"implied_outcome\": \"Approximately $19 billion in FY2027. Management's period-specific visibility is the strongest forecast input; Wolfe's estimate implies about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026 (1.3 million × about $5,000), but is a single-product, different-period downside check rather than a replacement estimate for total FY2027 CPU revenue.\",\n     \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to “nearly $20 billion” in FY2027 total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3 (at most $20 billion); the rung 2 boundary is revenue above $20 billion. The strongest estimate remains about $19 billion: the $20 billion target without a period and the $200 billion market opportunity do not establish a higher FY2027 run-rate, while Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera-unit estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for that product in calendar 2026, a material but not directly comparable downside check. June deliveries and OpenAI's planned Q3 deployment support sales, but disclose no volume that would move the forecast above $20 billion. This is consistent with v14's approximately $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue at about $19 billion is a subset and does not exceed the whole.\",\n     \"why_changed\": \"The implied outcome remains about $19 billion and the key period-specific reference remains “nearly $20 billion.” The additional market and competitive evidence does not quantify a revision to NVIDIA's FY2027 CPU revenue forecast or move the implication across a rung boundary.\",\n     \"cites\": [\n      \"e443\",\n      \"e666\",\n      \"e668\",\n      \"e723\",\n      \"e724\",\n      \"e794\",\n      \"e793\"\n     ],\n     \"pushes\": [\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e442\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e443\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e475\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e489\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e584\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e596\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e597\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e612\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e664\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e665\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e666\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e667\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e668\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e676\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e679\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e681\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e689\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e694\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e715\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e723\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e724\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e725\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e793\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e794\",\n       \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"evidence_id\": \"e795\",\n       \"push\": \"none\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    \"quotes of the target sources\": {\n     \"e667\": \"- e667 [third_party_view; CNBC.com; original source Gartner analyst Kevin Knox (market-share figures are reported by CNBC.com) ; from gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c 2026-07-21] Gartner analyst Kevin Knox said AMD was gaining share in server CPUs; AMD reportedly held about 33% of the market and Intel 66.8%.\\n    Quote: Gartner analyst Kevin Knox said that AMD is currently the company to beat in enterprise AI server CPUs. AMD reportedly holds about 33% of the market for server CPUs, and Intel has 66.8%, but AMD is gaining share and has deep relationships with hyperscalers.\",\n     \"e694\": \"- e694 [third_party_view; WIRED; original source WIRED 2026-07-21; from gnews:e4b99d721a7a9c46cefa4038 2026-07-21] AMD significantly increased its share of the data-center CPU market over the two years preceding 2026-07-21.\\n    Quote: Over the past two years, AMD has significantly grown its share of the market for CPUs used in data centers.\",\n     \"e715\": \"- e715 [third_party_view; SDxCentral; original source Gartner ; from gnews:2838708eba4178345bbc70e8 2026-07-21] Gartner named AMD the frontrunner in the increasingly diverse enterprise AI server CPU market; no period was specified.\\n    Quote: AMD finds itself as the Gartner-crowned frontrunner for an enterprise AI server CPU market that’s becoming increasingly diverse.\",\n     \"e794\": \"- e794 [third_party_view; Investing.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:55b3b854d30ecb73679c2088 2026-07-22] AMD estimates EPYC 9965 delivers approximately 2.4 times the rack-level throughput of NVIDIA Vera in a modeled 100kW deployment, and projects EPYC 6 at roughly 3.3 times Vera's total throughput.\\n    Quote: AMD estimates its EPYC 9965 (Turin) chip delivers approximately 2.4x the rack-level throughput of NVIDIA's Vera baseline in a modeled 100kW deployment, with the next-generation EPYC 6 (Venice) projected to extend that advantage to roughly 3.3x total throughput, according to BofA's characterization of AMD's own estimates.\"\n    },\n    \"reader 1\": \"intended (the required claim is stated in substance and a listed source is used for it)\",\n    \"reader 2\": \"omitted (the required claim is not stated as frozen)\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"decide\": \"for each item: intended | omitted | unsupported (and valid_support true|false), by the rule text as written; one line of reasons each.\"\n }\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r '.[\\\"Part B\\\"] | to_entries[] | if (.value|type)==\\\"object\\\" then {id:.key,target:.value.target,answer:(.value.answer|del(.cites,.pushes)), source_uses: (.value as \"'$v | {cites: [$v.answer.cites[] | select(. as $id | $v.target.sources | index($id))], pushes: [$v.answer.pushes[] | select(.evidence_id as $id | $v.target.sources | index($id))]})} else .value end'\"' poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/adjudication-case.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r '.[\\\"Part B\\\"] | to_entries[] | if (.value|type)==\\\"object\\\" then {id:.key,target:.value.target,answer:(.value.answer|del(.cites,.pushes)), source_uses: (.value as \"'$v | {cites: [$v.answer.cites[] | select(. as $id | $v.target.sources | index($id))], pushes: [$v.answer.pushes[] | select(.evidence_id as $id | $v.target.sources | index($id))]})} else .value end'\"' poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/adjudication-case.json\"","aggregated_output":"### Part B (48 answers)\nEach answer is read against `probe-targets.json`.\n- **Evidence targets.** One class each, judged by the claim each cited or pushed entry is used for:\n  - *intended*: the claim is stated, an entry in `sources` is used for it, and no other entry is used for it without carrying it;\n  - *unsupported*: stated, with no entry in `sources`. An entry in `partial` alone leaves the exact claim unsupported, but is not a\n    wrong attribution;\n  - *misattributed*: an entry is used for the claim while its quote does not carry it. This counts even if the entry supports a\n    different claim in the same answer, or a correct source is also cited;\n  - *omitted*: the claim is not stated.\n  - The class is judged on the target's `required` claim against `sources`. An `optional` detail stated without its own source is\n    unsupported for that detail only, recorded, and does not change the class.\n  - When more than one class applies, misattributed comes first, then intended, then unsupported, then omitted. A wrong source with\n    no correct one is misattributed.\n  - Apart from the class, each draw records **valid support**: the required claim is stated and an entry in `sources` is used for\n    it, whether or not a wrong source is also used. The material-loss rules count draws without valid support, whatever their class.\n  A `markers` search in the answer's text flags the reader's attention; the hand reading decides.\n- **Placement targets.**\n  - *correct*: the id appears only in its own field;\n  - *absent*: not named;\n  - a substitute evidence id carrying no claim the answer states is *misattributed*.\n- **Ambiguous targets** (two acceptable sources, or an unclear fact) are reported and count neither way.\n- **A second, blind reader** (Codex) reads the 48 against the frozen targets. A disagreement goes to a third, source-backed read whose\n  class decides.\n\n\njq: error (at poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/adjudication-case.json:1076): Cannot iterate over null (null)\n","exit_code":5,"status":"failed"}}
