{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"jq '.[\\\"Part B\\\"].items | to_entries[] | . as {key:\"'$key,value:$value} | {id:$key,target:$value.target,answer:($value.answer|del(.cites,.pushes)),source_uses:{cites:[$value.answer.cites[]? | select(. as $id | $value.target.sources | index($id))],pushes:[$value.answer.pushes[]? | select(.evidence_id as $id | $value.target.sources | index($id))]},quotes:$value[\"quotes of the target sources\"]}'\"' poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/adjudication-case.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"jq '.[\\\"Part B\\\"].items | to_entries[] | . as {key:\"'$key,value:$value} | {id:$key,target:$value.target,answer:($value.answer|del(.cites,.pushes)),source_uses:{cites:[$value.answer.cites[]? | select(. as $id | $value.target.sources | index($id))],pushes:[$value.answer.pushes[]? | select(.evidence_id as $id | $value.target.sources | index($id))]},quotes:$value[\"quotes of the target sources\"]}'\"' poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/adjudication-case.json\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"id\": \"old-3:6458|1|e2769\",\n  \"target\": {\n    \"required\": \"NVIDIA expects Vera deployed by every major hyperscaler, with shipments underway to lead partners\",\n    \"sources\": [\n      \"e3083\"\n    ],\n    \"partial\": [],\n    \"near_misses\": [\n      \"e2767\",\n      \"e2768\"\n    ],\n    \"material\": false\n  },\n  \"answer\": {\n    \"reference\": \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook is the relevant market/company reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion); no separate consensus figure is supplied.\",\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"Approximately $25 billion or more for FY2027: about $20 billion of guided standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue already exceeding $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as a run-rate.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"The market/company reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU expectation, which sits at rung 2. The evidence implies roughly $25 billion or more in total CPU revenue: the standalone outlook plus Grace CPU revenue already above a $5 billion trailing-12-month run-rate. That reaches the rung 1 boundary if the Grace run-rate carries through FY2027. Vera production, shipments, and named customer demand support the outlook; AMD's performance claims and server-CPU competition are downside risks but do not quantify a reduction that would put the total below $25 billion.\",\n    \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 1, but the newly supplied AMD comparison quantifies a reported Vera performance disadvantage (including a claimed 20% advantage for AMD) and makes the competitive downside more concrete. It does not provide a quantified sales or revenue reduction, so the implied total remains around $25 billion or more.\"\n  },\n  \"source_uses\": {\n    \"cites\": [\n      \"e3083\"\n    ],\n    \"pushes\": [\n      {\n        \"evidence_id\": \"e3083\",\n        \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"quotes\": {\n    \"e3083\": \"- e3083 [party_statement; TechCrunch, quoting NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO 2026-08-26; from gnews:d949d26fe875780037064b8a 2026-08-26] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera shipments were already underway to lead partners, including Oracle and SpaceXAI, and NVIDIA expects deployment by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\\n    Quote: Kress said Wednesday that Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by “every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM, with shipments already underway to our lead partners,” which include Oracle and SpaceXAI.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n  }\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"old-3:6458|2|e2769\",\n  \"target\": {\n    \"required\": \"NVIDIA expects Vera deployed by every major hyperscaler, with shipments underway to lead partners\",\n    \"sources\": [\n      \"e3083\"\n    ],\n    \"partial\": [],\n    \"near_misses\": [\n      \"e2767\",\n      \"e2768\"\n    ],\n    \"material\": false\n  },\n  \"answer\": {\n    \"reference\": \"NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the market/company reference and sits at rung 2.\",\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"About $25 billion or somewhat above: approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue for FY2027 plus Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as a run-rate.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits at rung 2. The implied total is about $25 billion or somewhat above: the $20 billion standalone outlook plus Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. That crosses the $25 billion boundary for rung 1. Vera being in full production and shipments underway support the outlook; the AMD performance claims are a counterweight but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA’s forecast.\",\n    \"why_changed\": \"The rung and key implied figure remain the same as the previous conclusion: approximately $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue plus Grace’s more-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month run-rate. The additional deployment detail does not size or revise FY2027 revenue, and the AMD performance evidence does not quantify a change to NVIDIA’s outlook.\"\n  },\n  \"source_uses\": {\n    \"cites\": [\n      \"e3083\"\n    ],\n    \"pushes\": [\n      {\n        \"evidence_id\": \"e3083\",\n        \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"quotes\": {\n    \"e3083\": \"- e3083 [party_statement; TechCrunch, quoting NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO 2026-08-26; from gnews:d949d26fe875780037064b8a 2026-08-26] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera shipments were already underway to lead partners, including Oracle and SpaceXAI, and NVIDIA expects deployment by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\\n    Quote: Kress said Wednesday that Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by “every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM, with shipments already underway to our lead partners,” which include Oracle and SpaceXAI.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n  }\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"old-3:6458|3|e2769\",\n  \"target\": {\n    \"required\": \"NVIDIA expects Vera deployed by every major hyperscaler, with shipments underway to lead partners\",\n    \"sources\": [\n      \"e3083\"\n    ],\n    \"partial\": [],\n    \"near_misses\": [\n      \"e2767\",\n      \"e2768\"\n    ],\n    \"material\": false\n  },\n  \"answer\": {\n    \"reference\": \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU guidance; this reference sits at rung 2. No separate market consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.\",\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus a carried-forward Grace CPU run-rate above $5 billion (reported on a trailing-12-month basis) implies total FY2027 CPU revenue of at least about $25 billion, placing the forecast at rung 1.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU guidance, which sits at rung 2. The implied total is at least about $25 billion: the $20 billion standalone forecast is distinct from Grace, whose trailing-12-month CPU revenue already exceeded $5 billion; carrying that run-rate into FY2027 puts total CPU revenue at or above the rung-1 threshold. Vera production, shipments and customer commitments support delivery of the standalone forecast. AMD's performance claims and the challenge of taking share are counterweights, but they do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA's guidance or to Grace's run-rate. Recent AWS and FY2028 evidence strengthens the demand and execution context but does not justify raising the FY2027 estimate beyond this implied figure.\",\n    \"why_changed\": \"The rung and implied FY2027 figure remain at least about $25 billion, but new evidence adds material execution and demand context: NVIDIA described Vera shipments to lead partners and AWS's 2-million-GPU deployment alongside Vera CPUs, and projected FY2028 standalone CPU revenue above $40 billion. Those items support the outlook without changing the FY2027 quantitative anchor.\"\n  },\n  \"source_uses\": {\n    \"cites\": [\n      \"e3083\"\n    ],\n    \"pushes\": [\n      {\n        \"evidence_id\": \"e3083\",\n        \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"quotes\": {\n    \"e3083\": \"- e3083 [party_statement; TechCrunch, quoting NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO 2026-08-26; from gnews:d949d26fe875780037064b8a 2026-08-26] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera shipments were already underway to lead partners, including Oracle and SpaceXAI, and NVIDIA expects deployment by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\\n    Quote: Kress said Wednesday that Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by “every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM, with shipments already underway to our lead partners,” which include Oracle and SpaceXAI.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n  }\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"linked-1:8777|3|e693\",\n  \"target\": {\n    \"required\": \"AMD competition in data-center CPUs (share gains or performance claims)\",\n    \"sources\": [\n      \"e667\",\n      \"e694\",\n      \"e715\",\n      \"e794\"\n    ],\n    \"partial\": [],\n    \"near_misses\": [\n      \"e689\"\n    ],\n    \"material\": false\n  },\n  \"answer\": {\n    \"reference\": \"NVIDIA management's “nearly $20 billion” FY2027 total CPU revenue visibility is the market/company reference and sits in rung 3; rung 2 begins above $20 billion.\",\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"Approximately $19 billion in FY2027. Management's period-specific visibility is the strongest forecast input; Wolfe's estimate implies about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026 (1.3 million × about $5,000), but is a single-product, different-period downside check rather than a replacement estimate for total FY2027 CPU revenue.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to “nearly $20 billion” in FY2027 total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3 (at most $20 billion); the rung 2 boundary is revenue above $20 billion. The strongest estimate remains about $19 billion: the $20 billion target without a period and the $200 billion market opportunity do not establish a higher FY2027 run-rate, while Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera-unit estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for that product in calendar 2026, a material but not directly comparable downside check. June deliveries and OpenAI's planned Q3 deployment support sales, but disclose no volume that would move the forecast above $20 billion. This is consistent with v14's approximately $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue at about $19 billion is a subset and does not exceed the whole.\",\n    \"why_changed\": \"The implied outcome remains about $19 billion and the key period-specific reference remains “nearly $20 billion.” The additional market and competitive evidence does not quantify a revision to NVIDIA's FY2027 CPU revenue forecast or move the implication across a rung boundary.\"\n  },\n  \"source_uses\": {\n    \"cites\": [\n      \"e794\"\n    ],\n    \"pushes\": [\n      {\n        \"evidence_id\": \"e667\",\n        \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"evidence_id\": \"e694\",\n        \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"evidence_id\": \"e715\",\n        \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"evidence_id\": \"e794\",\n        \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"quotes\": {\n    \"e667\": \"- e667 [third_party_view; CNBC.com; original source Gartner analyst Kevin Knox (market-share figures are reported by CNBC.com) ; from gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c 2026-07-21] Gartner analyst Kevin Knox said AMD was gaining share in server CPUs; AMD reportedly held about 33% of the market and Intel 66.8%.\\n    Quote: Gartner analyst Kevin Knox said that AMD is currently the company to beat in enterprise AI server CPUs. AMD reportedly holds about 33% of the market for server CPUs, and Intel has 66.8%, but AMD is gaining share and has deep relationships with hyperscalers.\",\n    \"e694\": \"- e694 [third_party_view; WIRED; original source WIRED 2026-07-21; from gnews:e4b99d721a7a9c46cefa4038 2026-07-21] AMD significantly increased its share of the data-center CPU market over the two years preceding 2026-07-21.\\n    Quote: Over the past two years, AMD has significantly grown its share of the market for CPUs used in data centers.\",\n    \"e715\": \"- e715 [third_party_view; SDxCentral; original source Gartner ; from gnews:2838708eba4178345bbc70e8 2026-07-21] Gartner named AMD the frontrunner in the increasingly diverse enterprise AI server CPU market; no period was specified.\\n    Quote: AMD finds itself as the Gartner-crowned frontrunner for an enterprise AI server CPU market that’s becoming increasingly diverse.\",\n    \"e794\": \"- e794 [third_party_view; Investing.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:55b3b854d30ecb73679c2088 2026-07-22] AMD estimates EPYC 9965 delivers approximately 2.4 times the rack-level throughput of NVIDIA Vera in a modeled 100kW deployment, and projects EPYC 6 at roughly 3.3 times Vera's total throughput.\\n    Quote: AMD estimates its EPYC 9965 (Turin) chip delivers approximately 2.4x the rack-level throughput of NVIDIA's Vera baseline in a modeled 100kW deployment, with the next-generation EPYC 6 (Venice) projected to extend that advantage to roughly 3.3x total throughput, according to BofA's characterization of AMD's own estimates.\"\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"Part C\": {\n    \"linked-1:8776\": {\n      \"decision\": \"confirmed (b)\",\n      \"reasons\": \"v12 #2 calls the $393 billion estimate Bloomberg/Wall Street’s, and v12 #3 calls it Bloomberg’s. Both cite e669 for that figure. Its quote credits Wall Street’s average estimates provided by Yahoo Finance and does not identify Bloomberg. The unsupported Bloomberg attribution therefore appears in 2 of 3 v12 answers. v12 #1 and all three v7 answers cite e195, whose quote explicitly credits data compiled by Bloomberg.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"Part B\": {\n    \"old-3:6458|1|e2769\": {\n      \"class\": \"omitted\",\n      \"valid_support\": false,\n      \"reasons\": \"The answer mentions Vera shipments and named customer demand but leaves out NVIDIA’s expectation of deployment by every major hyperscaler, which e3083 states.\"\n    },\n    \"old-3:6458|2|e2769\": {\n      \"class\": \"omitted\",\n      \"valid_support\": false,\n      \"reasons\": \"The answer says Vera is in full production and shipments are underway, but leaves out both the lead partners and the expected deployment by every major hyperscaler stated in e3083.\"\n    },\n    \"old-3:6458|3|e2769\": {\n      \"class\": \"omitted\",\n      \"valid_support\": false,\n      \"reasons\": \"The answer states that Vera shipments are underway to lead partners and mentions AWS’s deployment, but leaves out NVIDIA’s broader expectation of deployment by every major hyperscaler stated in e3083.\"\n    },\n    \"linked-1:8777|3|e693\": {\n      \"class\": \"omitted\",\n      \"valid_support\": false,\n      \"reasons\": \"The answer refers generally to competitive evidence without stating AMD’s data-center CPU share gains or performance claims. The e794 citation and the listed pushes do not state the required claim in the answer.\"\n    }\n  }\n}"}}
